### =========================================================
### Auth allow-list testing (PublicRoute / isPublicRoute change)
###
### Goal: prove the new explicit allow-list behaves EXACTLY like the
### old "GET is public by default, except compatibility" behavior --
### same routes public, same routes protected, nothing flipped by
### accident. Also stress-tests pathMatches() against real path-param
### routes (e.g. /movies/{id} vs the literal /movies/search,
### /movies/top-rated -- these must NOT be confused with each other).
###
### Run top to bottom. If re-running a second time, step 1/2 will fail
### with DuplicateUsernameException/DuplicateEmailException since the
### usernames/emails are hardcoded -- tweak them (e.g. auth_test_a2)
### and rerun from the top.
### =========================================================


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### SETUP: two users, A and B, plus a movie + review to exercise
### the routes that need real data (reviews, watchlist, following).
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 1. Register user A
POST http://localhost:8080/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "auth_test_a",
  "email": "auth_test_a@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

> {%
client.global.set("tokenA", response.body.token);
client.global.set("userIdA", response.body.user.id);
%}


### 2. Register user B
POST http://localhost:8080/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "auth_test_b",
  "email": "auth_test_b@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

> {%
client.global.set("tokenB", response.body.token);
client.global.set("userIdB", response.body.user.id);
%}


### 3. Import a movie (protected route, needs token) to have a real
### internal movie id available for the rest of this file
POST http://localhost:8080/movies/import
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

{
  "tmdbId": 27205
}

> {%
client.global.set("movieId", response.body.id);
%}


### 4. A reviews the movie (so /movies/{id}/reviews and
### /users/{id}/reviews have real data to return)
POST http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

{
  "rating": 8
}

> {%
client.global.set("reviewId", response.body.id);
%}


### 5. A adds the movie to their watchlist (so /users/{id}/watchlist
### has real data)
POST http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

{
  "movieId": {{movieId}}
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### PART 1 -- PUBLIC ROUTES, NO TOKEN SENT
### Every one of these MUST succeed (200/201) with zero Authorization
### header. If any of these come back 401, isPublicRoute() rejected
### something it shouldn't have -- pathMatches() or the PUBLIC_ROUTES
### list itself is wrong.
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 6. GET / (root sanity check)
### Expect: 200 + "Hello from Javalin!"
GET http://localhost:8080/


### 7. GET /movies
### Expect: 200 + array of movies
GET http://localhost:8080/movies


### 8. GET /movies/search?title=... -- must NOT be swallowed by the
### /movies/{id} pattern (this exact confusion was the original
### Phase 4 route-ordering bug; pathMatches() needs to get this right
### via segment-count + literal-segment matching, not registration order)
### Expect: 200 + array of TMDB results
GET http://localhost:8080/movies/search?title=inception


### 9. GET /movies/top-rated -- same concern as #8, a literal two-segment
### path that must not be treated as /movies/{id} matching "top-rated"
### Expect: 200 + array of {movie, averageRating}
GET http://localhost:8080/movies/top-rated


### 10. GET /movies/{id} -- the actual wildcard-segment case
### Expect: 200 + the imported movie
GET http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}


### 11. GET /movies/{id}/reviews
### Expect: 200 + array containing A's review (with username, per the
### ReviewResponse DTO)
GET http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews


### 12. GET /users/search?username=... -- must NOT be swallowed by
### /users/{id} (same literal-vs-wildcard concern as #8/#9)
### Expect: 200 + array containing auth_test_a and auth_test_b
GET http://localhost:8080/users/search?username=auth_test


### 13. GET /users/{id}
### Expect: 200 + user A's public info
GET http://localhost:8080/users/{{userIdA}}


### 14. GET /users/{id}/reviews
### Expect: 200 + array containing the review from step 4
GET http://localhost:8080/users/{{userIdA}}/reviews


### 15. GET /users/{id}/watchlist
### Expect: 200 + array containing the watchlist entry from step 5
GET http://localhost:8080/users/{{userIdA}}/watchlist


### 16. GET /users/{id}/following
### Expect: 200 + [] (A isn't following anyone yet)
GET http://localhost:8080/users/{{userIdA}}/following


### 17. GET /users/{id}/followers
### Expect: 200 + [] (nobody follows A yet)
GET http://localhost:8080/users/{{userIdA}}/followers


### 18. POST /users -- already exercised in step 1/2 above, but
### confirming again explicitly as its own allow-list entry with a
### throwaway user
### Expect: 201 + {user, token}
POST http://localhost:8080/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "auth_test_c",
  "email": "auth_test_c@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}


### 19. POST /login -- already implicitly proven by every {{tokenA}}/
### {{tokenB}} use above, confirming directly with no Authorization header
### Expect: 200 + {"token": string}
POST http://localhost:8080/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "auth_test_a@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### PART 2 -- THE ROUTE THAT MATTERS MOST: compatibility.
### This is a GET route that is DELIBERATELY NOT on PUBLIC_ROUTES.
### It must behave the opposite of every GET above: reject with no
### token, succeed with one.
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 20. GET /users/compatibility/{otherId} -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401 (proves the allow-list correctly excludes this GET route,
### same as the old isCompatibilityRoute carve-out used to)
GET http://localhost:8080/users/compatibility/{{userIdB}}


### 21. GET /users/compatibility/{otherId} -- WITH TOKEN, not mutually
### following B yet
### Expect: 403 NotMutualFollowException (proves the token WAS accepted
### and execution reached the actual business logic, not another 401)
GET http://localhost:8080/users/compatibility/{{userIdB}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### PART 3 -- PROTECTED ROUTES, NO TOKEN SENT
### Every one of these MUST fail with 401. If any of these succeed,
### something that should require auth is accidentally matching a
### PUBLIC_ROUTES entry.
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 22. POST /movies/import -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
POST http://localhost:8080/movies/import
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tmdbId": 155
}


### 23. POST /movies/{id}/reviews -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
POST http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rating": 5
}


### 24. POST /users/watchlist -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
POST http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "movieId": {{movieId}}
}


### 25. POST /users/following -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
POST http://localhost:8080/users/following
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "followeeId": {{userIdB}}
}


### 26. PUT /users/username -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
PUT http://localhost:8080/users/username
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "should_not_work"
}


### 27. PUT /users/email -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
PUT http://localhost:8080/users/email
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "should_not_work@example.com"
}


### 28. PUT /users/password -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
PUT http://localhost:8080/users/password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "should_not_work"
}


### 29. PUT /movies/{id}/reviews/{reviewId} -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
PUT http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rating": 3
}


### 30. DELETE /users -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
DELETE http://localhost:8080/users


### 31. DELETE /users/watchlist/{entryId} -- NO TOKEN (entryId can be
### bogus, we're testing that auth is checked BEFORE the route even
### looks up the entry)
### Expect: 401 (not 404 -- proves auth is checked first)
DELETE http://localhost:8080/users/watchlist/999999


### 32. DELETE /users/following/{followeeId} -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
DELETE http://localhost:8080/users/following/{{userIdB}}


### 33. DELETE /movies/{id}/reviews/{reviewId} -- NO TOKEN
### Expect: 401
DELETE http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### PART 4 -- SAME PROTECTED ROUTES, WITH A VALID TOKEN
### Spot-checks (not every single one) that a real token still gets
### through fine -- proves the allow-list isn't accidentally blocking
### legitimate authenticated traffic.
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 34. PUT /users/username -- WITH TOKEN
### Expect: 200 + updated user
PUT http://localhost:8080/users/username
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

{
  "username": "auth_test_a_renamed"
}


### 35. POST /users/following -- WITH TOKEN (A follows B)
### Expect: 201 + follow relationship
POST http://localhost:8080/users/following
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

{
  "followeeId": {{userIdB}}
}


### 36. DELETE /movies/{id}/reviews/{reviewId} -- WITH TOKEN
### Expect: 200 + {"deleted": true}
DELETE http://localhost:8080/movies/{{movieId}}/reviews/{{reviewId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}


### ---------------------------------------------------------
### PART 5 -- OPTIONS PREFLIGHT (sanity check, not strictly an
### isPublicRoute() concern since OPTIONS is handled before the
### allow-list check runs, but worth confirming nothing regressed)
### ---------------------------------------------------------

### 37. OPTIONS /users/compatibility/{otherId} -- the exact route that
### needs auth for GET should still let an OPTIONS preflight through
### untouched (browsers send this automatically, no Authorization header)
### Expect: 200 (or whatever Javalin's CORS plugin normally returns here --
### the point is NOT 401)
OPTIONS http://localhost:8080/users/compatibility/{{userIdB}}
